Tutor Feedback

The tutor recognised several strengths.

The group clearly understood the Airbnb business problem and framed price prediction in a strategic context. Relevant variables were selected, and the report included both a linear regression baseline and neural-network approaches.

The group also applied appropriate preprocessing, train-test splitting, evaluation metrics and permutation importance.

However, the tutor identified important limitations.

Weak Justification of Deep Learning

The report did not critically justify why neural networks were suitable for the tabular Airbnb dataset.

A neural network can capture non-linear relationships, but this does not automatically make it the best model for structured tabular data. The report should have compared the neural network with additional alternatives such as:

random forest;
gradient boosting;
regularised regression;
XGBoost;
support-vector regression.

The marginal improvement over linear regression was not critically evaluated.

Limited Validation

The project depended mainly on one train-test split.

This reduced confidence in the robustness of the results. A stronger approach would have included:

cross-validation;
repeated train-test splits;
hyperparameter tuning;
sensitivity analysis;
uncertainty estimates.
Weak Model Performance

The reported R² was approximately 0.14.

This meant that the model explained only a limited proportion of the variation in Airbnb prices. The group acknowledged low explanatory power, but the business consequences were not examined deeply enough.

A weak model may provide some exploratory insight, but it should not be recommended as a dependable pricing system without additional data and validation.

Limited Analysis of Architecture

The report included two neural-network architectures, but the design choices were not sufficiently explained.

The report should have justified:

the number of layers;
the number of neurons;
the activation functions;
the learning rate;
the optimiser;
the regularisation method;
the number of epochs;
the stopping rule.
Dataset Limitations

The group recognised price skewness, missing features and overfitting, but the analysis remained descriptive.

A stronger critique should have examined:

omitted variables;
seasonal effects;
location granularity;
host reputation;
property quality;
temporal changes;
sample representativeness;
possible bias in listing availability;
impact of extreme prices.
Business Implications

The report discussed practical implications, but the recommendations were not sufficiently operationalised.

A business recommendation should be linked directly to:

a model result;
the size and reliability of the effect;
the relevant Airbnb stakeholder;
the action that should be taken;
the limitation affecting that decision.
Visualisations and Referencing

The figures and tables were labelled, but some visualisations were only briefly interpreted. Figures 3 and 4 in particular required stronger integration into the discussion.

The referencing broadly followed Harvard style, but some technical decisions were unsupported and minor inconsistencies remained.

What I Learned

The project taught me that completing a complex model does not guarantee a strong analysis.

I learned that model complexity must be justified and that weak performance must be discussed honestly. I also learned that business usefulness depends on validation, interpretability and practical implementation rather than on model novelty.

In future group projects, I would introduce:

a clear validation plan;
a baseline-comparison table;
documented hyperparameter tuning;
independent code checking;
stronger feature justification;
detailed interpretation of every figure;
an evidence-based business recommendation section.
